# Shipped into every module repository by demo/push-content.sh, alongside
# .tflint.hcl and the issue templates. The module repositories are generated
# artefacts; edit this file, not theirs.
#
# It exists because of a real failure. `ci/gates.sh` runs `terraform init`
# inside a checkout, and CI, the nightly and the module agent all commit from a
# checkout they have just run the gates in. The google provider it downloads is
# a 116.88 MB binary, and a push carrying it is rejected outright:
#
#   remote: error: GH001: Large files detected.
#   remote: error: File .terraform/providers/.../terraform-provider-google_v6.50.0_x5
#           is 116.88 MB; this exceeds GitHub's file size limit of 100.00 MB
#
# The agent no longer depends on this file — `stage_all` in agent/entrypoint.py
# excludes `.terraform/` at the staging boundary, because the runner cannot
# assume anything about a repository's contents. This is the other half: it
# protects the humans and the workflows, which do a plain `git add`.
.terraform/

# State never belongs in a module repository. These modules are consumed by
# other configurations; the only root that applies them keeps its state in GCS.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
crash.log
crash.*.log

# Local input and CLI configuration, which is per-developer by definition.
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json
.terraformrc
terraform.rc

# Deliberately NOT ignored: .terraform.lock.hcl.
#
# The usual advice — "do not commit a lock file in a shared module" — is about
# consumers, and it is correct about them: when this module is called by
# another configuration, only the *caller's* lock file is consulted, so the one
# here is inert downstream. That makes committing it harmless there, not
# useless here.
#
# Here, the repository is also a root working directory in its own right. `make
# lint`, `make test`, ci/gates.sh and the nightly all run `terraform init` and
# `terraform test` at this level, and for those runs the lock is the only thing
# pinning which provider version was actually exercised and verifying its
# checksums. Committed, a provider bump is a reviewable diff and CI is
# reproducible; ignored, every run silently resolves whatever is newest, and
# the day a provider release breaks a module the failure arrives with no diff
# to point at. The cost is that the pin can go stale without anyone noticing —
# which the nightly, re-initialising against the version constraints in
# versions.tf, is the thing that surfaces.
